  "query_preview": "..\n\nKey Message 1...Dry weather with a day to day warming trend through \nSaturday.\n\nHigh pressure will build east across the eastern Great Lakes \ntonight, with another mainly clear and cool night. Lows will drop \nback into the low to mid 50s again in most areas, with mid 40s for \nthe cooler Southern Tier valleys and Tug Hill region. Expect river \nvalley fog across the Southern Tier late tonight through Friday \nmorning.\n\nSurface high pressure will linger from the eastern Great Lakes to \nNew England Friday through Saturday, with a continuation of dry \nweather. There is just a slight chance of a late afternoon shower or \nthunderstorm Saturday as a weak shortwave interacts with diurnal \ninstability and lake breeze boundaries, but this would remain very \nisolated if anything develops.\n\nOngoing warm advection will continue to support a day to day warming \ntrend, with highs in the lower 80s Friday and mid 80s Saturday for \nlower elevations away from the immediate lakeshores.\n\nKey Message 2...Unsettled weather returns Sunday and continues the \nfirst half of next week.\n\nA strong mid/upper level ridge will remain centered over and just \neast of the 4-corners Sunday through the middle of next week. A \nsteady parade of shortwaves will traverse the northern edge of the \nridge position, allowing a longwave trough to gradually dig and \nexpand across eastern Canada and adjacent areas of the Great Lakes \nand New England. \n\nEach passing shortwave will bring a chance of a few showers and \nthunderstorms, starting Sunday and lasting through at least the \nmiddle of next week. While the forecast will feature a chance of \nrain each day, much of the time will still be rain free with very \nuneven coverage of showers and thunderstorms. Northwest flow around \nan upstream ridge can often be an active pattern for localized \nsevere thunderstorms across the Great Lakes and New England, so this \npotential will need to be watched as this period draws closer.",
